{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794818312,"nonce":"s+JYKlCNIQvkKv7ryUr5/EA8Mho=","title":"nAIR needs its own type table: HashLink's types collapse what the typed program distinguishes","message":"The module interface answers type questions in HashLink's terms (TypeKind, the runtime's hl_type). Those stay, because the runtime builds objects, reflection and the GC from them. But the typed program says more than they can hold, and an AIR value carries only a TypeRef into the HL table, so what is collapsed there is gone before any pass sees it. Cited by 1760a826c7a1f8164e7b6b9399e22f9f5e5ecfd5a79f2b84c45cd475da2e7cad.\n\nWhere ash_nair/src/build.rs collapses a type (checked in the code):\n- An anonymous structure with no fields is Dyn (anon_type); a recursive one is Dyn at the point of recursion (rec_cache). An anonymous structure with fields is a Virtual, which is also what an interface becomes, so \"implements\" survives only as ObjType.interfaces.\n- MONO and DYNAMIC are Dyn. An unconstrained type parameter is Dyn. An abstract is its underlying type, or Dyn with none.\n- Array\u003cT\u003e is the class for T's representation: every reference element type is ArrayObj, so the element type is gone.\n- Null\u003cT\u003e of an already nullable T is T.\n- Class FINAL and field FINAL are never read; INLINE and DYNAMIC are read only to decide method-table slots. The TAST records no optional or read-only mark on an anonymous structure's fields (AnonFieldRec carries METHOD only), so the file would need extending for those.\nSome of this is what genhl does too; it is still data the direct compile has and bytecode never did.\n\nWhat it is worth (static counts from ash_nair's new `opportunities` bin; one per place in the source, not per execution; 12 programs, small):\n- Declared final: none. 0 final classes and 0 final fields in all twelve. A flag-driven fact gives nothing; the fact has to be inferred.\n- Fields never written outside a constructor, over the program's own classes: 20 of 36 in the five benchmarks that have fields (BinaryTrees 3 of 3, Mandelbrot 5 of 5, DeltaBlue 10 of 19, NBody 2 of 8, FieldAccess 0 of 1). They take 125 of the 213 field reads (59%): BinaryTrees 5 of 5, Mandelbrot 27 of 27, DeltaBlue 71 of 133, NBody 22 of 46. These are loads LICM and GVN cannot move past a call or a store today.\n- Classes nothing in the file extends: every class in the benchmarks but three of DeltaBlue's twelve. Exact receivers for devirtualization, type tests and casts.\n- Anonymous structures: 2, 1 and 0 object literals and 4, 4 and 3 field reads on an anonymous structure in the three programs that use them, and no field read on a Dynamic. It is typed structure access, which the runtime does through a virtual's field lookup. With the shape kept, a read can be a typed load where the producers are known, and a literal that does not escape can be scalar-replaced.\n- Interface calls: 1 in each of two tests; devirtualizable in a closed world.\nCaveats: the \"never written\" test assumes a closed world (no Reflect or Dynamic writes, no hot reload), and counts a write inside a closure in a constructor as a constructor write.\n\nDecision to make. Keep HlType as the runtime's type; add our own table behind TypeRef, where an entry has a runtime projection (an HL type) and what the typed program says (anonymous shape, abstract identity, interface relation, element type, nullability, final/exact, immutable fields). Two entries may project to one HL type, so Dynamic and {x:Int} are different TypeRefs that share a representation where HashLink does. ModuleInfo gains repr(ty) (default: itself) and the facts as methods that default to \"unknown\", so a bytecode module is unchanged; type_kind answers for the projection, and the runtime's descriptors are built per projection. The cost is that TypeRef equality gets finer, which can only make a pass more conservative. The alternative, side tables keyed by value, cannot tell two values of one TypeRef apart, which is the case that loses data.","files":null}]}